1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
I'm Tim Jakes and I am Scoutmaster with Troop 93 in Westchester, Pennsylvania.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:11,000
This edition of Scoutmaster Podcast is sponsored by backers like me

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:13,000
And now the old Scoutmaster.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:41,000
Hey, everybody, this is Clark Green. It's summertime and so our next few podcasts feature on-core presentations

5
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:47,000
from our archives, and this week is an interview with somebody who kind of defies categorization.

6
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:53,000
His name is Andrew Skirka and I think it's safe to say that he is the most incredible

7
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:56,000
long-distance backpacker and hiker in living memory.

8
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:02,000
Andrew's book, The Ultimate Hikers Gear Guide, is a really amazing resource for anybody

9
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:04,000
who's going backpacking or hiking.

10
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:09,000
As always, this podcast is only possible because of the generosity of the folks who become

11
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:12,000
ScoutmasterCGcom backers.

12
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:16,000
Go to ScoutmasterCGcom, click the support link at the top of the page.

13
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:20,000
You'll find out how you can become a ScoutmasterCGcom backer.

14
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:23,000
We'll be back with our regular schedule of podcasts in a few weeks.

15
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:26,000
Until then, let's get started, shall we?

16
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:49,000
Andrew Skirka has won a lot of attention for his solo long-distance backpacking trips.

17
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:55,000
For instance, he hiked a 4,700-mile six-month expedition in Alaska and the Yukon,

18
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:58,000
which would be like a lifetime achievement for most of us,

19
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:01,000
but that was basically one of the shorter ones.

20
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:07,000
He covered 6,875 miles in seven months following the Great Western Loop

21
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:11,000
in the United States, but that's still not the longest one.

22
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:17,000
He walked 7,775 miles- just saying that makes my feet hurt-

23
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:21,000
in an 11-month sea-to-sea route across the US,

24
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:27,000
and he figures that in the last decade or so he's covered 30,000-plus miles

25
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:31,000
and that's the equivalent of traveling 1.2 times around the Earth's equator.

26
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:36,000
He's been named Adventurer of the Year by both Outside and the National Geographic

27
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:40,000
Adventure Magazines, as well as Person of the Year by Backpacker Magazine.

28
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:44,000
He's also the author of the recently published Ultimate Hiker's Gear Guide

29
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:46,000
from National Geographic,

30
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:50,000
And if you look at your March April edition of Scouting Magazine,

31
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:52,000
there he is right there on the cover.

32
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:55,000
I'm happy to welcome Andrew to the Scoutmaster Podcast.

33
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:56,000
Good morning, Andrew. How are you?

34
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:58,000
Hey, good morning. Thanks very much for having me.

35
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:01,000
Well, no, I really appreciate you spending the time.

36
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:04,000
How in the world did you end up doing this?

37
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:07,000
I think I sort of stumbled into it.

38
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:13,000
I never set out with the intention of making this into a lifestyle,

39
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:16,000
but I did my first long-distance trip

40
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:18,000
One. I was still in college. It was 2002..

41
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:23,000
I did the Appalachian Trail and then, after graduating, I did that sea-to-sea trip

42
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:26,000
and I think things just sort of escalated from there

43
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:31,000
And I was fortunate that I had never developed any extensive habits

44
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:34,000
or taken on any extensive responsibilities

45
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:39,000
and that kind of allowed me to be like a dirtbag hiker, if you will,

46
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:42,000
Kind of like a dirtbag climber, but I was hiking instead

47
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:47,000
and slowly over time I was able to build it into something

48
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:49,000
that was economically sustainable as well.

49
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:53,000
We're talking with volunteer scout leaders and we're all parents.

50
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:57,000
So you are getting ready to graduate from college-

51
00:03:57,000 --> 00:03:58,000
You were at Duke right,

52
00:03:58,000 --> 00:03:59,000
Correct?

53
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:04,000
And you decide that you're going to go and do a through-hike of the Appalachian Trail.

54
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:06,000
Is that, if I have the timeline right there,

55
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:08,000
Yeah, I get it before I graduate.

56
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:10,000
It's actually between semesters-

57
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:13,000
My son's name is Andrew, by the way,

58
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:17,000
And he came to us in the fall semester of his junior.

59
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:18,000
He says: you know what?

60
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:23,000
I think? I'd like to go and spend three or four months on the Appalachian Trail.

61
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:27,000
How did your parents react to that when you told them that you had this great idea?

62
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:30,000
The Appalachian Trail was part of our larger plan,

63
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:33,000
So the economy at the time was pretty bad.

64
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:36,000
It was just difficult to find anything worth doing for the summer,

65
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:42,000
So I kind of pitched it as something I could do this summer,

66
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:45,000
that I could sort of knock off my bucket list

67
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:48,000
and that way, when the economy is a little better

68
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:53,000
and my odds of finding something worth doing were better,

69
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:55,000
I would be available for it.

70
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:57,000
And they were thrilled about this.

71
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:00,000
They weren't thrilled but they were okay with it.

72
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:04,000
The plan made sense then

73
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:08,000
And they just didn't expect that when I did the Appalachian Trail,

74
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:11,000
that that wasn't going to be enough.

75
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:15,000
So the trip that they really, the trip that they really got upset about,

76
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:19,000
was that sea to sea trip, because that was, you know,

77
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:26,000
instead of doing something with my $150,000 education,

78
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:31,000
I was instead, you know, in their view,

79
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:38,000
sort of pissing it away by taking on this. you know,

80
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:42,000
I don't know how they were characters, useless, dangerous,

81
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:45,000
Useless, dangerous, crazy,

82
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:47,000
Yeah.

83
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:53,000
But I think my feeling on it was that I was at the one point in my life

84
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:55,000
where I really could do something like this.

85
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:57,000
I was young, I didn't have any responsibilities,

86
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:01,000
I hadn't committed to a career, to a woman, to a home,

87
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:03,000
I hadn't done any of that stuff.

88
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:04,000
So I was going to do anything like that.

89
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:06,000
That was the time

90
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:10,000
And I stuck with that decision because I had made the realization

91
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:14,000
that these lifestyle decisions are mine to make

92
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:18,000
And there's a lot of pressure from society, from parents,

93
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:21,000
from peers, to take that very conventional track

94
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:25,000
which is, you know, your college degree, job,

95
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:28,000
house or wife, house, kids,

96
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:30,000
Right,

97
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:32,000
And there's a lot of pressure to do that.

98
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:35,000
And I had made this realization that if I wanted to make

99
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:37,000
a different decision I could do that,

100
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:42,000
And you know. so the message I see in that for us folks is:

101
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:45,000
listen to your kids carefully,

102
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:48,000
Listen to what their plans are, what they're thinking about,

103
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:53,000
And hey, you know, down the road, look what can happen.

104
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:57,000
The traditional way out is not the only way out,

105
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:00,000
And Andrew's a real good example of that.

106
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:04,000
I'd also add, if I could, I think I'd also add that

107
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:08,000
I think any experience in which your child is putting themselves

108
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:13,000
out of their comfort zones and is having an opportunity to grow

109
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:17,000
will probably be a good thing in one way or another.

110
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:19,000
Absolutely, I absolutely agree with you

111
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:22,000
And I think scouting we try, we kind of major in that,

112
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:25,000
putting people outside their comfort zone in a lot of ways.

113
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:30,000
So you talk about the first real backpacking trip. you went on

114
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:33,000
as being that. you left. you set out from Springer Mountain

115
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:35,000
in Georgia on the Appalachian Trail.

116
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:37,000
How did that go?

117
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:39,000
Oh, it was bad.

118
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:41,000
It was bad.

119
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:43,000
So it was my first congressman's trip.

120
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:45,000
It was my first backpacking trip,

121
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:52,000
And now I describe myself as a camper by default,

122
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:55,000
which is a backpacker that takes too much,

123
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:58,000
because they're basically packing their fears.

124
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:00,000
They justify a lot of your decisions on the ground

125
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:02,000
of what if or just in case,

126
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:04,000
And they just don't know.

127
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:07,000
I also shared a lot of conventional gear,

128
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:10,000
which is because I just didn't know any better

129
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:14,000
And I thought that what the industry was telling me

130
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:16,000
and what an REI catalog was telling me,

131
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:18,000
that that was the right thing.

132
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:23,000
And then finally I had. no, I wasn't able to use any gear

133
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:28,000
that required any special thought or skill to use.

134
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:30,000
Like everything had to be foolproof.

135
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:33,000
So I wasn't capable of setting up the tarp,

136
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:35,000
So I had to use a dev wall pen.

137
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:43,000
I didn't know how to. I didn't know how to select a good campsite.

138
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:46,000
I didn't know how to pack a bag.

139
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:48,000
I didn't know how to pack a bag correctly.

140
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:51,000
So it was like it was necessary that I had like some big,

141
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:54,000
you know bombproof suspension pack where it didn't really matter

142
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:55,000
how I loaded it,

143
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:57,000
It was in there and it all sort of fit at the same.

144
00:08:57,000 --> 00:08:58,000
I didn't know any skills.

145
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:01,000
So that was kind of like three main problems.

146
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:05,000
And unfortunately, a lot of beginner and, to that matter,

147
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:08,000
I think, still a lot of intermediate backpackers are like that

148
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:11,000
And their experience is just so miserable

149
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:15,000
that their hiking experience is so miserable

150
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:17,000
that they end up just wanting to camp

151
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:19,000
because they just they can't enjoy hiking.

152
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:22,000
I think that your experience that you had

153
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:25,000
is played out in a slightly smaller scale,

154
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:29,000
like hundreds and hundreds of times every year

155
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:31,000
They give this a shot and then they,

156
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:33,000
tens of thousands of times,

157
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:34,000
Tens of thousands of times-

158
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:35,000
Yeah,

159
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:38,000
They're 20,000 kids who go through film on every summer

160
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:41,000
and probably of those, probably 19,000 of them

161
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:43,000
probably are not doing it right.

162
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:47,000
You go into detail about that experience

163
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:51,000
and kind of the evolution that you went through

164
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:54,000
as far as the selection of gear and everything

165
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:58,000
and your philosophy and your approach to these trips

166
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:01,000
in the Ultimate Hikers Gear Guide-

167
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:05,000
And I have read the book,

168
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:06,000
I've looked at it.

169
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:08,000
Folks, you will have seen, yesterday

170
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:11,000
I posted a review of the book in advance

171
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:14,000
of being able to talk to Andrew here on the podcast.

172
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:16,000
It's really a game changer.

173
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:18,000
I think it's a great piece of work

174
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:23,000
And I think it's going to make things really

175
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:25,000
really work for people.

176
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:29,000
If they'll go and look at the book and follow your advice,

177
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:32,000
I think we're going to see a whole lot more successful

178
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:34,000
backpackers developed out of it.

179
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:35,000
I certainly hope so.

180
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:38,000
The way I've been presenting it is basically,

181
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:42,000
I think I look at it as a $20 investment.

182
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:44,000
I've traveled, as you said

183
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:47,000
in the introduction, I've gone like 30,000 miles.

184
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:52,000
I've made a lot of regrettable purchases of gear.

185
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:55,000
I've made a lot of mistakes and suffered as a result

186
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:57,000
because I chose the wrong stuff

187
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:00,000
or I didn't have the skills to use it properly,

188
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:02,000
And I've also made some really good decisions

189
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:04,000
that I've learned a lot along the way. in the book

190
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:05,000
I share that

191
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:08,000
The focus is on backpackers who want to enjoy hiking.

192
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:11,000
I think that there's plenty of information about there.

193
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:14,000
for backpackers who want to enjoy camping,

194
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:17,000
They can take it like read a book by Knowles.

195
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:20,000
Knowles specializes in camping.

196
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:23,000
They're basically car campers that are mobile.

197
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:27,000
There isn't that much information on a mass scale

198
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:30,000
about if you actually want to enjoy hiking,

199
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:31,000
which is different.

200
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:32,000
You're moving,

201
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:35,000
You have to carry all of your stuff,

202
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:38,000
And that's kind of been what I've specialized in

203
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:40,000
is trying to figure out how to enjoy

204
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:43,000
the walking aspect of all these trips I've gone on.

205
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:46,000
That's like this connection that people don't make.

206
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:48,000
That's why I think the book is a real game-changer.

207
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:52,000
that comes at it from the idea of trying to define,

208
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:54,000
like, where you are

209
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:58,000
And then if you want to get to the point of

210
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:01,000
being able to backpack comfortably, you know

211
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:02,000
here's what you do.

212
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:05,000
If you want to end up being an ultimate hiker,

213
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:09,000
if you want to really challenge some big experiences,

214
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:11,000
you know here's what you end up doing.

215
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:17,000
And you know I read the part in your introduction

216
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:23,000
that you were kind of aiming at trying to do

217
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:25,000
the complete walker- Colin Fletcher's-

218
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:28,000
the complete walker for this century-

219
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:29,000
And I think you made it

220
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:33,000
Well. I appreciate that reference.

221
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:35,000
Yeah, that's definitely my objective.

222
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:38,000
Colin's book is:

223
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:40,000
They're awesome books.

224
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:43,000
They changed the way that people backpack.

225
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:49,000
Unfortunately, we now have our disposal

226
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:52,000
New gear, new supplies, new techniques

227
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:55,000
that allow us to do it differently and to do it better,

228
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:59,000
And that was my idea in writing the book.

229
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:01,000
Yeah, well, I think you were successful

230
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:04,000
in kind of helping to redefine

231
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:06,000
exactly what we're doing out there

232
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:08,000
and how we make it happen. well,

233
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:11,000
One of the things that I first saw about you

234
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:13,000
was the YouTube video

235
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:16,000
And it really made me anxious to talk to you,

236
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:20,000
And this video is when you encounter

237
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:22,000
the porcupine caribou herd,

238
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:23,000
Right,

239
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:25,000
And where were you at the time?

240
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:27,000
I was up in the Yukon, Arctic,

241
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:32,000
So like basically far north,

242
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:35,000
Far northwestern Canada.

243
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:38,000
So you have, like, this moment-

244
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:40,000
I don't know exactly how to describe this moment-

245
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:42,000
a moment of enlightenment or clarity

246
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:45,000
or some kind of transcendent moment.

247
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:47,000
Can you share that with us?

248
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:49,000
Can you explain what happened?

249
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:51,000
Yeah, I still struggle to explain it, to be honest,

250
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:54,000
But basically I've been so worn down

251
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:57,000
by what I was going through

